14: `
<p>The Champions League combines a shared league table with elimination football. Its league phase is not a domestic-style competition in which everybody plays everybody, and it is no longer the old set of four-team groups. Understanding who each team faces, and what finishing positions buy, makes the changing table much more useful.</p>
<p>This guide describes the league-phase system introduced in 2024/25 and retained in the current format. Qualification into the tournament is a separate subject, explained in <a href="/articles/13">How Champions League Qualification Works</a>.</p>
<h2>A league of 36, with eight fixtures each</h2>
<p>Thirty-six clubs share one league-phase table. Each plays eight different opponents, with four fixtures at home and four away. A team does not play a return fixture against each of those opponents during this stage. The draw uses seeding pots and constraints to construct the schedule.</p>
<p>Eight opponents from a field of 35 possible opponents means schedules are not identical. Two clubs next to each other in the table may have faced quite different combinations of teams. Points still decide progress, but an analyst should examine the actual fixtures before concluding that two performances were equivalent.</p>
<p>Imagine one side taking four points from two difficult away matches while another wins twice at home. The latter has the better immediate points return. Whether it has the stronger long-term outlook depends partly on which games remain. That is a reason to add context, not to replace the official table with a subjective one.</p>
<h2>Points and ranking</h2>
<p>A win is worth three points, a draw one and a defeat none. Eight wins would produce 24 points. The final ranking uses points first, then UEFA's ordered tiebreakers, beginning with goal difference and goals scored. Further criteria apply when those do not separate teams.</p>
<p>Do not import a domestic league's head-to-head rule into this table. Some tied teams will not even have played each other. A live ranking can also change quickly when several matches are being played simultaneously, especially where goal difference separates clubs around a qualification boundary.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/articles/15">goal difference guide</a> explains the arithmetic. For an exact tie involving several clubs, use UEFA's current regulations rather than assuming that the first familiar statistic is decisive.</p>
<h2>Three outcomes from the league phase</h2>
<ul><li>Places 1–8 advance directly to the round of 16.</li><li>Places 9–24 enter the knockout phase play-offs.</li><li>Places 25–36 are eliminated from European competition for that season; there is no league-phase drop into the Europa League.</li></ul>
<p>The play-offs are therefore an extra hurdle, not another name for the round of 16. Sixteen clubs contest them and eight winners join the eight direct qualifiers. This creates the 16-team field for the next round.</p>
<p>Finishing eighth rather than ninth avoids two competitive fixtures and the risk of elimination in them. Finishing 24th rather than 25th preserves a route to the trophy. Those boundaries can make matches involving teams well below first place particularly important on the final league-phase night.</p>
<h2>How the knockout stages work</h2>
<p>The play-offs, round of 16, quarter-finals and semi-finals are contested over two legs. The scores from both matches are added to determine the aggregate score. Under the current format, away goals are not an extra tiebreaker. An aggregate tie is resolved with extra time and, if required, penalties after the second leg.</p>
<p>Consider an invented tie in which Club A wins 2–1 at home and loses 1–0 away. The aggregate score is 2–2. Club B does not advance merely because it scored an away goal in the first match. That older interpretation would produce the wrong outcome.</p>
<p>League-phase ranking also affects knockout seeding and the bracket. Exact pairing and home-leg arrangements should be read from the current season's draw rules. Do not assume the competition simply redraws every remaining club into an unrestricted pool after every round.</p>
<h2>The final is a single match</h2>
<p>The final decides the trophy in one match at the selected venue. If the teams are level after normal time, extra time and then penalties can determine the winner. There is no return leg to recover from a narrow defeat.</p>
<p>This changes the tactical problem. In a two-leg tie, a coach can consider the aggregate situation and a future home match. In the final, all remaining opportunity is contained in that evening. The same scoreline can therefore lead to different choices about substitutions and risk.</p>
<h2>Reading a qualification prediction responsibly</h2>
<p>No points target should be described as a permanent guarantee of eighth or 24th place. The distribution of results across all 36 clubs determines the boundary. A projection can estimate what might be enough; a confirmed result requires the actual mathematics and tiebreakers.</p>
<p>Use <a href="/matches">Matches</a> for the available fixture information and the Champions League selection on <a href="/standings">Standings</a> for table context. Then ask which boundary a team is chasing and which fixtures it has left. That is more informative than treating every upward move as the same achievement.</p>
`
